WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.960
When it comes to action movies, 2021 has&nbsp;
been going pretty well so far. Out of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.600
those theatrical releases and streaming debuts,&nbsp;
however, it's hard to know which are the cream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.240
of the crop. Have no fear, though — these&nbsp;
are the best action movies of 2021 so far.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.720
In March 2021, after much demand from the&nbsp;
franchise's fans, DC and Warner Bros. released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.840
director Zack Snyder's original vision for&nbsp;
the Justice League's first live-action film.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:31.200
Following the 2017 release of Joss Whedon's&nbsp;
much-derided Franken-movie, no one knew quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:36.960
what to expect from this new take on Snyder's&nbsp;
original vision. But the end results are strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.520
featuring more fleshed out story arcs for each&nbsp;
member of the team and a villain with more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:47.600
relatable motivations. But Zack Snyder's Justice&nbsp;
League truly delivers with its action scenes.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:53.360
To die-hard Snyder fans, the director's talent&nbsp;
for action will come as no surprise. For anybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:59.120
who hadn't yet experienced his particular brand&nbsp;
of artfully framed, gleefully ridiculous action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:03.760
however, Justice League is a superb&nbsp;
demonstration of what he does best.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:09.600
The setup for Godzilla vs. Kong is simple:&nbsp;
Godzilla attacks humanity unprovoked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.560
so the humans call upon Kong to lead them&nbsp;
to a source of energy that can defeat him.&nbsp;

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.880
During the journey, the Terror of Tokyo&nbsp;
crosses paths with the eighth wonder of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.880
the world — and they throw down to see&nbsp;
who gets to be king of the monsters.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:27.520
While the movie received mixed reviews on&nbsp;
release, critics have largely praised its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:33.200
Kong on Zilla action. Director Adam Wingard's&nbsp;
previous movies, You're Next and The Guest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.360
married a number of genres with some&nbsp;
seriously effective action scenes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:42.160
And Wingard's greatest success in&nbsp;
Godzilla vs. Kong is his keen ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:47.840
to plot out those fight scenes and make every&nbsp;
punch, bite, and nuclear breath blast count.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:52.240
And sure, the critics have taken issue with&nbsp;
Godzilla vs. Kong's plotting and character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:56.640
development, but that's not really the point&nbsp;
of this movie, is it? As a wise man once said:

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:58.320
"Let them fight."

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.080
Watching an action movie can sometimes&nbsp;
feel like visiting a familiar diner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.840
You know the menu, you know what you&nbsp;
like, and you know what not to order.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:13.360
So when a movie like Nobody comes along, any&nbsp;
fan of the genre knows they're in for a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:19.360
specific viewing experience. Produced by one&nbsp;
half of the John Wick brain trust, David Leitch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:25.760
and starring funny man Bob Odenkirk, Nobody&nbsp;
nonetheless proved to be certifiably awesome.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:29.360
This movie brings such relish&nbsp;
to what it is attempting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.880
from the performances to the fight&nbsp;
choreography to the plot itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:38.160
that it easily stands out in the long line of&nbsp;
action-thriller franchises that came before it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:44.320
Most of that success is due to Odenkirk himself&nbsp;
— who, in a stroke of genius, plays Hutch Mansell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:51.040
like a recovering addict; one who has gleefully&nbsp;
broken a long, cold-turkey run of non-violence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.280
Throw in a wonderful supporting cast and&nbsp;
some deft direction from Ilya Naishuller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:01.600
and you've got one of those rare movies that&nbsp;
truly transcends the tried-and-tested formula.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:07.360
World War 2 is having a bit of a genre moment&nbsp;
right now, and movies blending extreme war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:13.680
violence with sci-fi/horror action have become&nbsp;
a cottage industry — think 2018's Overlord or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.080
The Outpost franchise. What makes Shadow&nbsp;
in the Cloud unique to this niche category,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.240
however, is its perspective and&nbsp;
focus on the women in the war effort.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:26.640
Shadow tells the story of Chloe Grace Moretz's&nbsp;
Flight Officer Maude Garret, and the secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:32.240
mission that has her hitching a ride on a B-52&nbsp;
bomber in the midst of Japanese occupied airspace.

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:37.520
The first half of the movie basically confines&nbsp;
Moretz to the bottom turret of the plane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:44.880
Her isolation is in one of the worst places to be&nbsp;
in an aircraft — outside and underneath it. Pair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:50.080
that with the sexist verbal abuse she receives&nbsp;
from the male crew over the radio, and the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:56.160
builds a highly specific brand of suspense not&nbsp;
often seen on the silver screen. But things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:01.360
really get crazy when spoiler-heavy details&nbsp;
reveal themselves, allowing the movie to dive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:06.480
into some B-movie action that wouldn't feel out&nbsp;
of place in an empty movie theater in the 1980s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:11.600
If you haven't already, give this one a shot —&nbsp;
it's as silly as anything and well worth a watch.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.720
The Fast and the Furious movies aren't about&nbsp;
car chases. They aren't about wild stunts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:20.720
either, or muscled-up dudes punching each&nbsp;
other. Okay, so maybe they're about those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:25.280
things a little — but at the end of the&nbsp;
day, this franchise is all about one thing:

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.600
"I don't have friends. I got family."

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:33.280
That's especially true in F9, in&nbsp;
which everybody's favorite street&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:37.920
racer Dominic Toretto butts heads with&nbsp;
his estranged brother, Jakob. Obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:43.120
the result is a whole bunch of car chases, wild&nbsp;
stunts, and muscled-up dudes punching each other.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:49.360
Luckily, F9 is also one of the best installments&nbsp;
in a truly wild and incredible franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:55.440
It hails the return of Justin Lin, the director&nbsp;
who reinvigorated the series with Tokyo Drift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:58.880
and created a new direction&nbsp;
for the franchise with parts 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:03.920
5, and 6. Here, the filmmaker expertly&nbsp;
blends the melodrama with the spectacle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:10.880
giving long-time fans of the franchise everything&nbsp;
they want and more. If you're looking for a fun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:16.640
fast-paced, and heartfelt time at the movies,&nbsp;
F9 is the perfect action film for you.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:21.840
Cinematically speaking, 2021 has been a fantastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.080
year for Zack Snyder. In addition to&nbsp;
wowing critics and fans alike with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:32.480
unadulterated take on Justice League, the man gave&nbsp;
Dave Bautista a machine gun, sent him to Sin City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:38.320
and had him shoot down a whole bunch of&nbsp;
zombies. Talk about peak action cinema.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:43.360
Army of the Dead takes place in a world where a&nbsp;
zombie uprising has been confined to Las Vegas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:49.280
The undead shuffle past slot machines, poker&nbsp;
tables... and a vault filled with $200 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.840
And so zombie-killing veteran&nbsp;
Scott Ward is hired to assemble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:57.680
a team of mercenaries to make their&nbsp;
way into Vegas and retrieve the cash.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:02.160
If they succeed? It's the ultimate&nbsp;
jackpot. If not? Well, you know...

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:07.120
Things get complicated when it turns out the&nbsp;
government plans on nuking Vegas off the face of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:12.880
the Earth. Things get even more complicated when&nbsp;
Scott's estranged daughter tags along. And things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:18.560
get even more complicated when the zombies reveal&nbsp;
themselves to be far more than your ordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:24.560
ghouls. Army of the Dead: it's silly, it's loud,&nbsp;
it's gory. What more could you possibly want?

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:31.360
It took way too long for Natasha Romanoff to get&nbsp;
her own movie — but now that Black Widow is here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:36.400
it turns out the final result was definitely&nbsp;
worth the wait. This Marvel action flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:41.360
works as a prequel, picking up right after the&nbsp;
airport battle in Captain America: Civil War.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:44.800
After betraying Team Tony and&nbsp;
letting Steve Rogers escape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:49.840
Natasha is on the run, trying to lay low&nbsp;
and figure out her new, post-Avengers life.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:54.400
But when the super spy crosses paths with&nbsp;
her adoptive sister, Yelena Belova, she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:59.840
realizes it's time to take care of some unfinished&nbsp;
business. Before long, she has reunited with her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:05.520
old adoptive family to take down some villains&nbsp;
and blow some stuff up. You know, the usual.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:09.200
More than anything, though, Black&nbsp;
Widow is about what it means to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:13.120
from a fractured family — and it's also&nbsp;
a surprisingly thoughtful look at guilt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:18.080
as Natasha is still struggling to wipe all&nbsp;
that red from her ledger. In addition to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:24.640
those engaging themes and some impressive action&nbsp;
scenes, Johansson gives a fantastic performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:29.600
bolstered by a hilariously goofy David&nbsp;
Harbour and a charmingly snarky Pugh.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:34.960
It's a sad farewell to Natasha herself, but&nbsp;
at least Marvel has sent her off in style.

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:40.000
Let's be honest — the original&nbsp;
Suicide Squad wasn't great.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:43.840
"So that's it, we're some kind of suicide squad?"

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:50.000
It has a lousy 26% critical score over on&nbsp;
Rotten Tomatoes, and even the audience score&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:56.560
is a lackluster 59% — the lowest in the DCEU&nbsp;
so far. So why is the sequel any different?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:01.360
In a sentence: it's all down to the magic of&nbsp;
James Gunn. The director who made everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:05.920
fall in love with a talking raccoon and a&nbsp;
dancing tree was the perfect guy to shepherd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:11.680
this ragtag band of antiheroes to success.&nbsp;
And sure enough, with The Suicide Squad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:17.840
Gunn injects the DC universe with a&nbsp;
heavy dose of quippy, crazy, gory fun.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:22.320
Of course, Gunn hasn't totally forgotten about&nbsp;
the original film, as he's brought over some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:27.440
the best characters from the first installment —&nbsp;
but he also brings in a whole new rogues' gallery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:31.040
none of whom are guaranteed to make&nbsp;
it even to the movie's halfway point.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:35.840
As for the plot, the titular squad is sent down to&nbsp;
a South American island to stop a nefarious scheme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:41.040
called "Project Starfish." And yes, that's exactly&nbsp;
what it sounds like. By the time it's all said and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:46.640
done, Gunn has offered up a Dirty Dozen-style&nbsp;
war movie with plenty of gags, plenty of guts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:51.680
and a near-perfect Rotten Tomatoes score.&nbsp;
The only lingering question… When's Part 3?

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:57.120
When you think about legendary action stars,&nbsp;
there are any number of names that might jump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:01.760
to mind. But what about Karen Gillan? She's&nbsp;
Nebula in the Marvel Cinematic Universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:06.480
Ruby Roundhouse in the Jumanji franchise, and&nbsp;
famously cut her teeth in the world of Doctor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:12.000
Who. But with Gunpowder Milkshake, Gillan&nbsp;
truly proves her action star bona fides.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:17.040
This action thriller finds Gillan playing Sam,&nbsp;
an assassin working for a mysterious organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:21.280
called the Firm. But when a dangerous&nbsp;
mission goes south, Sam decides to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:26.240
turn on the Firm and protect an 8-year-old&nbsp;
girl, drawing the wrath of her employers.

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:28.720
If you love the worldbuilding&nbsp;
of the John Wick franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:33.120
then Gunpowder Milkshake will be right up&nbsp;
your alley. It also features some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:38.720
craziest action scenes you'll see all year,&nbsp;
and shines a spotlight on the ladies, too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:44.080
with women supporting women as they batter down&nbsp;
bad guys and mow down hit-men. Filled to the brim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:50.960
with neon visuals and madcap violence, Gunpowder&nbsp;
Milkshake is a genuine treat for the senses.

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:58.720
It's a tale as old as time... or at least as&nbsp;
old as 1993. Somebody finds themselves trapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:04.080
in the same day, and no matter what happens, they&nbsp;
wake right back up on the very same morning and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:08.880
repeat the same 24 hours all over again.&nbsp;
Groundhog Day properly kicked off the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:13.120
loop genre, but a number of movies have&nbsp;
played with the concept over the years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:17.040
None, though, were quite so&nbsp;
action-packed as Boss Level.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:23.040
Starring the world's most underrated action star,&nbsp;
Frank Grillo, Boss Level finds Roy Pulver stuck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:28.560
in a particularly painful time loop. Every&nbsp;
day, he gets killed. No matter what he does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:33.760
he wakes up and then, at some point or another,&nbsp;
he wounds up dead. Naturally, this doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:38.880
sit well with Mr. Pulver, and he sets out to&nbsp;
crack some skulls, evade his imminent death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:44.960
and figure out how he got trapped in this crazy&nbsp;
day. With some solid direction by Joe Carnahan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:50.560
and a great villainous turn from Mel Gibson,&nbsp;
Boss Level is a fun spin on a familiar premise.

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:55.120
Who are the greatest actor-director&nbsp;
combos of all time? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:58.320
you've got Akira Kurosawa and&nbsp;
Toshiro Mifune , of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:04.080
and John Ford and John Wayne. Or maybe you'd say&nbsp;
Martin Scorsese and Robert De Niro. And sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:08.960
they're all great. But Toshiro Mifune never&nbsp;
had a gunfight with Post Malone, did he?

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:12.640
Enter Guy Ritchie and Jason&nbsp;
Statham. From Snatch to Lock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:16.480
Stock, these two action icons&nbsp;
have made some of the craziest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:22.080
most intense movies of all time. And, in&nbsp;
2021, they treated us to Wrath of Man.

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:25.520
The movie stars Statham as Patrick Hill,&nbsp;
a security guard who keeps crooks from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:30.960
knocking off armored trucks. He's the new guy&nbsp;
on the job, but he's surprisingly good at it...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:36.160
a little too good, in fact. Naturally, Hill's&nbsp;
co-workers begin to suspect there's more to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:41.440
this rookie than meets the eye, and soon enough,&nbsp;
it's revealed that something sinister is going on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:47.520
Forget the plot, though — Wrath of Man is, most&nbsp;
importantly, filled with gunfire and explosions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:53.600
and features Statham at his action star best&nbsp;
here. And sometimes that's all you need.

